#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# list_density_plot

#% text
# `list_density_plot(m)` shows the numbers of the matrix m as colours: row k at height k, column j at x = j, with a colour bar giving their scale.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is `image("path")`, a PNG file with SVG and PDF copies next to it.

#% item
# The first row is at the bottom. The colours are drawn smoothly between the points of the matrix.

#% item
# The matrix needs at least two rows and two columns of equal length; an entry that is not a real number leaves its part of the picture empty.

#% item
# Options: `data_range: [[x0, x1], [y0, y1]]` places the first and last columns at x0 and x1 and the first and last rows at y0 and y1; `color_function` and `color_bar` work as for `density_plot`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A small matrix with its largest value in the middle:

#% cell
list_density_plot([[1, 2, 1], [2, 4, 2], [1, 2, 1]])
#% output
#= image("list_density_plot.files/list_density_plot-1.png")

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# A table of values placed on the square from 0 to 1:

#% cell
list_density_plot(table(sin(i/4)*cos(k/6), i, 30, k, 40), data_range: [[0, 1], [0, 1]], color_function: earth)
#% output
#= image("list_density_plot.files/list_density_plot-2.png")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `density_plot`, `raster`, `table`
